  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA cold start this morning with lows in the 20s will lead into \nanother dry day today with highs slightly warmer than yesterday.\nHighs will warm into the mid 40s with the help of some clearing\nskies pushing in from the southeast before increasing clouds \nand a wetter period from Thanksgiving through Friday arrives. A \nnegatively tilted trough pushes south into the northern Plains \nand Midwestern states from central Canada before becoming a \nmore closed low feature and pushing northeastward through the \nOhio River valley and into the northeastern portions of the \nCONUS later this week. Decent warm air advection associated with\nthis system for our area looks to keep precipitation mainly in \nthe liquid form beginning Thursday early for our southern \nportions of the area and will quickly spread northward across \nthe remainder of the area on Thursday.\n\nOvernight Thursday into Friday morning a few snowflakes may mix\nin but no snowfall accumulations are expected. However, \nrainfall totals for this system around an inch will be possible \nacross the southern parts of the CWA with a gradual downward \ntrend the further north you go with around a third of an inch \nfor our southern MI counties. High temperatures over the next \nseveral days look to remain in the mid to upper 40s and lows \nmainly in the 30s. Dry weather returns this Saturday briefly \nwith ridging in the wake of this first system moving through. \nAnother system pushes eastward across the central CONUS and into\nthe region and looks to bring another bout of light rainfall \nSunday into Monday morning.",
